# ##########################################################################
# THIS FILE DOES NOT PARSE AND IS NOT MEANT TO. It is the answer to
# "your effects are chatty -- tell me what that wants to be."
# ##########################################################################
#
# THE REAL THING, in blue-ribbon:
#
#   fetchParcelsIntersecting(polygon) pages an ArcGIS FeatureServer until the
#   server stops setting `exceededTransferLimit`. Osceola caps at
#   maxRecordCount: 2000; one 6-mile stream returns 839 parcels; the Pine
#   River (57 miles) returns far more. `fetchAllEsriPages` loops, accumulating
#   into one array. Iosco's Au Sable corridor returns 3,265 candidates, and
#   then fetchTownship is called ONCE PER CANDIDATE -- 3,265 sequential
#   requests to a shared statewide service, which reliably hit an HTTP/2
#   GOAWAY partway through, which is why retryOnTransportError exists.
#
# So one logical step, "get me the parcels in this corridor", is somewhere
# between 2 and 4,000 HTTP requests whose count is NOT KNOWN UNTIL THE SERVER
# SAYS SO.
#
# WHAT WEIR OFFERS, and how far it gets:
#
#   A cycle is recursion, terminated by a oneOf branch nothing routes back:
#
#     input: PageRequest
#     output:
#       oneOf:
#         - MorePages      # routed back to fetchParcelPage
#         - PagesComplete  # routed onward
#
#   This genuinely works, and it is nicer than the while loop: every page is a
#   logged, replayable token, and `resultOffset` is carried in the edge rather
#   than mutated in a local. The readme is right that the accumulator was
#   redundant with the log.
#
# WHERE IT STOPS WORKING -- three things, in increasing severity:
#
# 1. REASSEMBLY. I need every page's parcels as one collection. `gather`
#    "collects every instance of one edge descended from a SINGLE SPREAD".
#    A cycle is not a spread. The page count is not known when the loop
#    starts, so there is nothing to spread over. I can see no way to express
#    "gather everything this cycle produced" -- which means the one shape
#    weir offers for iteration cannot be rejoined, and an ETL that cannot
#    rejoin its pages has not done anything.
#
#    This is the single blocking gap for modelling blue-ribbon's real
#    workload rather than my one-parcel slice.
#
# 2. GRANULARITY. Even if reassembly worked: is one HTTP request one node
#    firing? At 3,265 township lookups per corridor that is 3,265 log entries
#    per run for ONE FIELD, plus a trace entry each, plus a pinned
#    implementation hash each. The log stops being an audit trail and becomes
#    the dominant cost of running the pipeline. I do not think you want
#    fetch-level granularity here, but the alternative -- one effect node that
#    internally makes 3,265 requests -- hides the chattiness at exactly the
#    place Principle 0 says to expose it, AND makes replay feed back one giant
#    recorded blob rather than 3,265 checkable ones.
#
#    WHAT I THINK IT WANTS TO BE: an effect whose declared cardinality is
#    `many`, performed by the host as a batch, recorded per-element, and
#    spread on return. i.e. the host is allowed to be chatty, the log is
#    per-element, and the topology shows ONE crossing.
#
#      effect: http
#      effectCardinality: many     # <-- does not exist
#      input: CorridorQuery
#      output:
#        many: RawParcelFeature
#
#    That keeps the boundary honest (one place the world gets in), keeps
#    replay granular, and lets the host own retry/backoff/pagination, which
#    is where that policy belongs anyway -- the effects spec already puts
#    "retries, timeouts, backoff" explicitly out of scope, but pagination is
#    not policy, it is how you obtain the data at all.
#
# 3. PARTIAL FAILURE. `retryOnTransportError` exists because request 1,847 of
#    3,265 fails. In weir that parcel's branch emits Failed<In>, and the run
#    stalls at the gather with residue. blue-ribbon's actual semantics are
#    "that parcel gets a null with a note saying why, and the corridor still
#    produces a card for the other 3,264". A gather that cannot tolerate a
#    missing element cannot express a batch job. What I want is a gather that
#    fires on quiescence with holes, and records the holes -- which is very
#    close to your `residue` concept, except reported INTO the payload rather
#    than to the run's exit code.
#
label: Fetch Parcel Page
description: NOT A REAL DECLARATION -- see the comment block above.
effect: http
input: PageRequest
output:
  oneOf:
    - MorePages
    - PagesComplete
